/// Chapter 19 of the guide, written from md-docs/about/how-reactivity-works.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page AboutHowReactivityWorks(path: "/about/how-reactivity-works", title: "How reactivity works", description: "How WebFluent's signals, derived values, effects and keyed lists work under the hood, and why what reads a value is what redraws.", layout: DocsShell("How reactivity works", kind: .explanation, chapter: t("nav.group.about"), blurb: "What happens between a click and the one text node it changes: signals, derived values, effects, branches and keyed lists, with no virtual DOM in between.", source: "md-docs/about/how-reactivity-works.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "what-a-page-compiles-to", label: "What a page compiles to"), Anchor(id: "a-signal-knows-who-reads-it", label: "A signal knows who reads it"), Anchor(id: "a-derived-value-is-an-effect-that-writes-a-signal", label: "A derived value is an effect that writes a signal"), Anchor(id: "one-change-one-ordered-run", label: "One change, one ordered run"), Anchor(id: "values-are-replaced-not-mutated", label: "Values are replaced, not mutated"), Anchor(id: "a-branch-tracks-its-condition-and-nothing-else", label: "A branch tracks its condition, and nothing else"), Anchor(id: "a-keyed-list-moves-rows-instead-of-rebuilding-them", label: "A keyed list moves rows instead of rebuilding them"), Anchor(id: "a-refresh-keeps-what-did-not-change", label: "A refresh keeps what did not change"), Anchor(id: "what-it-costs", label: "What it costs")], prevTo: "/about/why", prevLabel: t("ch.18"), nextTo: "/about/accessibility", nextLabel: t("ch.20"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This essay explains what WebFluent does when a value changes: how a `state` knows who reads it, why only those readers redraw, and how branches, lists and refreshed data keep the nodes they already drew.\n\nThe claim it defends: a page should be drawn once, and after that a change should touch exactly the nodes that read what changed. Everything below is the machinery that makes that true, and the price it charges.")

        Heading("What a page compiles to", id: "what-a-page-compiles-to").h2

        Markdown("This page has a value that changes, a value worked out from it, and a button that changes it.")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Count", description: "A counter.") {
    state count = 0
    derived double = count * 2
    Heading("Count").h1
    Text("{count} doubled is {double}")
    Button("Add one") { on click { count = count + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`wf build` turns it into one function that runs once, when the route is shown. Here it is, laid out for reading:")

        CodeBlock(#"function Page_Home(params) {
  const _count = WF.signal(0);
  const _root = document.createDocumentFragment();
  const _double = WF.computed(() => _count() * 2);
  _root.appendChild(WF.el("h1", { className: "wf-heading" }, "Count"));
  _root.appendChild(WF.el("p", { className: "wf-text" },
    () => `${_count()} doubled is ${_double()}`));
  const button = WF.el("button", { className: "wf-btn", type: "button" }, "Add one");
  button.addEventListener("click", () => { _count.set(_count() + 1); });
  _root.appendChild(button);
  return _root;
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("There is no render function that runs again, no tree of descriptions to compare with the last one, and no component that re-renders. The heading is a plain `<h1>` made once. The paragraph's text is the one thing that can change, and it is handed to `WF.el` as a function: that function is what will run again.")

        Heading("A signal knows who reads it", id: "a-signal-knows-who-reads-it").h2

        Markdown("`WF.signal(0)` returns a getter. Calling it returns the value, and, if an effect is running at that moment, adds that effect to the signal's list of readers. That is the whole trick of dependency tracking: nothing is declared, because reading is declaring.\n\nThe function the paragraph was given becomes an effect over one text node. Its first run reads `_count()` and `_double()`, so both now list it. When the button calls `_count.set(1)`, the signal compares the new value with the old one (`!==`), and, since they differ, runs the effects that read it. The text node gets its new text. The `<h1>`, the button and the rest of the page are not touched, because nothing about them read `count`.\n\nAn effect listens to exactly what its last run read. Each run takes it off every signal it was listed on, and the run lists it again. So an effect whose condition stopped reading a value stops running for that value, and the set of dependencies is always the true one, not one you wrote down.")

        Heading("A derived value is an effect that writes a signal", id: "a-derived-value-is-an-effect-that-writes-a-signal").h2

        Markdown("`derived double = count * 2` compiles to `WF.computed`: a signal of its own, kept current by an effect that reads `count`. When `count` changes, that effect runs, writes `double`, and the write runs `double`'s readers in turn. A derived value that works out to the same value as before (`count > 0` while `count` goes from 3 to 4) stops there: its signal compares, sees no change, and its readers do not run.\n\nTwo consequences follow:\n\n- A derived value is eager. It is worked out when what it reads changes, not when something reads it, so reading it costs nothing.\n- It cannot be assigned. It has one source, its expression, and the compiler refuses an assignment to it (`X01`).")

        Heading("One change, one ordered run", id: "one-change-one-ordered-run").h2

        Markdown("A change does not redraw at once and then again for every dependent; it collects every effect it reaches and runs them in one pass, oldest first. The order matters. A branch is made before what it draws, so when an `if` closes and a `derived` it guarded changes in the same click, the branch runs first, takes its contents away, and the effects inside it are disposed of before they can read a value that no longer makes sense for them.\n\nAn effect that writes what it reads comes back into the same pass. One that settles does so in a run or two. One that never settles would loop forever; the compiler refuses the ones it can see (`X02`), and the runtime stops any other after 100 runs, with a warning under `wf serve`.")

        Heading("Values are replaced, not mutated", id: "values-are-replaced-not-mutated").h2

        Markdown("A signal notices a change by comparing identities. If code pushed onto the list a `state` holds, the list would be the same object, and nothing would redraw. So WebFluent never lets that happen silently: `items[i].done = v`, `form.name = v` or `items.sort()` written as a statement compile to the state given a copy with that place changed. Reading `items.sort(…)` in an expression sorts a copy. You write the change the way you think of it, and the signal still sees a new value.")

        Heading("A branch tracks its condition, and nothing else", id: "a-branch-tracks-its-condition-and-nothing-else").h2

        Markdown("An `if` compiles to `WF.when`, an effect that reads only the condition. The branch's body is drawn with tracking turned off, so a value the body reads does not make the whole branch draw again; it makes the one node that reads it update, as anywhere else. The branch draws again only when the condition flips.\n\nEverything the body made belongs to the branch. Effects, timers from `every` and `after`, event listeners on the window, handles from `ref:`: each is created inside a scope, and when the branch leaves, the scope runs every cleanup it collected. A signal that outlives the branch, such as a store's state, has the branch's effects taken off its list, so it does not keep their nodes alive. That is why a page in WebFluent does not leak a timer when you navigate away from it: the page is a scope too.")

        Heading("A keyed list moves rows instead of rebuilding them", id: "a-keyed-list-moves-rows-instead-of-rebuilding-them").h2

        Markdown("This page has a list, a count worked out from it, and a branch.")

        CodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String, done: Bool = false }

page Todos(path: "/", title: "Todos", description: "A list that keeps its rows.") {
    state todos: [Todo] = [Todo(id: "1", title: "Milk"), Todo(id: "2", title: "Bread")]
    state draft = ""
    derived left = todos.filter(item => !item.done).length

    Heading("Todos").h1
    Text("{left} left")
    Input(bind: draft, label: "New todo")
    Button("Add") { on click { todos = todos.concat([Todo(id: uuid(), title: draft)])  draft = "" } }
    for todo in todos by todo.id {
        Checkbox(bind: todo.done, label: todo.title)
    }
    if left == 0 {
        Text("All done.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`for … by todo.id` compiles to `WF.each` with a key. Each row is drawn once and filed under its key. When the list changes, the runtime walks the new list: a key it has seen keeps its nodes and is moved into place if it moved; a new key is drawn; a key that is gone is disposed of and removed. A checkbox that has focus keeps it while rows are added or removed around it, because it is the same element.\n\nA keyed `for` is also *live*. Its body reads the item through an accessor, a small signal of its own, so a new value under the same key reaches the row's nodes in place: the label's text node updates, and nothing else in the row is redrawn. Without `by`, the runtime has no way to tell one row from another, and rebuilds the list whole on every change; the compiler warns when that would lose state (`U09`).")

        Heading("A refresh keeps what did not change", id: "a-refresh-keeps-what-did-not-change").h2

        Markdown("Data from a server makes the same promise harder to keep, because every response is new objects. A resource that asks again on an interval would otherwise hand the list a fresh array of fresh rows every 30 seconds.")

        CodeBlock(#"type Server { id: String, name: String, status: String }

page Servers(path: "/", title: "Servers", description: "Every server, kept current.") {
    resource servers: [Server] = fetch("/api/servers", on: .interval(30.seconds))
    Heading("Servers").h1
    match servers {
        loading { Text("Loading…") }
        error(e) { Text(e.message) }
        ready(list) {
            for s in list by s.id { Text("{s.name}: {s.status}") }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Before the answer to a refresh is stored, the runtime lays it over what was there: a row with the same `id` that is equal to the old one is replaced by the old object, and a list in which nothing changed is the old list. Then the ordinary machinery does the rest. The `ready` arm stays drawn, since the arm is still `ready`. The list, handed the same array, has nothing to do. A server whose status changed gives a new object under the same key, and its row's text node updates. An answer that changed nothing redraws nothing.")

        Heading("What it costs", id: "what-it-costs").h2

        Markdown("Fine-grained reactivity is not free. Its costs are the reasons for most of the rules you meet in the book:\n\n- **A value read once is read once.** A `state` that starts from `now` or `viewport` takes the value it had when the page opened and never moves (`X06`). Only a `derived`, or text that reads the value, follows it.\n- **Identity is the change.** A list changed in place behind the compiler's back, by your own script, is not a change. Hand a script an action, and let the action assign.\n- **Keys are your job.** The runtime cannot guess which row is which. A `by` that names what identifies the item, not its position, is what lets a row keep its focus and its state.\n\nIn return, a click costs what it changes and nothing more, a page needs no memoization to stay fast, and what you see in the source, the place a value is read, is the place the page updates.\n\nFor the language side of this, read [State and reactivity](/learn/07-state-and-reactivity) and [Control flow](/learn/09-control-flow). The functions named here are listed in the [runtime API](/reference/runtime).")
    }
}
